`forEach` doesn't know about promises. It calls your callback for each file and ignores the return value. Each `async` callback returns a promise as soon as it hits its first `await`, and `forEach` discards that promise. So the loop finishes right away, `console.log('done')` runs, and the `processFile` calls are still in flight.

To wait for all the files, collect the promises and await them.

**In parallel:**

```js
async function processAll(files) {
  await Promise.all(files.map((f) => processFile(f)));
  console.log('done');
}
```

**One at a time, in order:**

```js
async function processAll(files) {
  for (const f of files) {
    await processFile(f);
  }
  console.log('done');
}
```

Use `Promise.all` when the files are independent and you want speed. Use the `for...of` loop when order matters or you need to limit load, for example when each call opens a file handle or hits a rate-limited API.

One more difference: with `Promise.all`, the first rejection makes the whole call reject right away, though the other calls keep running. If you want every result whether or not some fail, use `Promise.allSettled` instead.
